WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.740
**Thunder**
**Thunder**

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:17.438
**Splash**

00:00:17.438 --> 00:00:17.458
**Splash**
**Splash**

00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:17.480
**Dog laugh**
**Music**

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.600
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:27.040
The show that's gone barking mad!

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.120
Today we're heading back into&nbsp;
the wonderful world of Bluey.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.480
The hit animated show coming to&nbsp;
us live from the land down under.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.800
Now in case you've missed out on this one,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.400
Bluey's all about a family&nbsp;
of dogs known as the Heelers.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.080
There's Bluey, her sister Bingo,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.080
her father Bandit, and her mum Chilli.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.360
Each 8 minute episode sees them&nbsp;
engaging in simple everyday adventures

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.120
like playing keepy-uppy with a balloon

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.040
or preparing for the family barbecue.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.960
All in a way that's super fun for&nbsp;
kids and super relatable for adults.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.800
This is great! They're learning a&nbsp;
lesson and we get the house clean!

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.920
But what's really made Bluey&nbsp;
iconic are the episodes

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.840
that focus on the wild imagination&nbsp;
games that the family plays.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.400
Which range from whale watching,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.760
to pretending there's a fairy&nbsp;
causing mischief in the house,

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.480
to their dad acting as a human claw machine.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.840
Insert coin for awesome prizes.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.000
Legitimately this is one of the best&nbsp;
animated kids shows that I've seen in years.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.760
Tackling all the joys and stresses&nbsp;
that are both childhood and parenting

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.720
in like the purest way possible.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.920
It is so good.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.760
True story, I started watching the&nbsp;
show with Ollie about a year ago

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.720
and while he's now graduated up&nbsp;
into the world of Gravity Falls,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.040
I am so proud of you buddy,

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.240
I'm still late night binging episodes of Bluey

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.160
and relating hard to Bandit.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.600
But the more I watch, the more&nbsp;
one question lingers in my mind.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.560
Is magic real in this world?

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.760
You see, the imagination on display&nbsp;
as the characters play their games

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.280
really goes to the next level.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.200
Like really!

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.120
These characters are incredibly&nbsp;
invested in their backyard adventures.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.480
Whether it's something like&nbsp;
a very aggressive puppet,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.100
We can handle this Mum!

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:50.760
Unicorse!

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.000
Ow! My back! My neck!

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.960
My back and my neck! Whiplash!

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:55.920
Call my lawyer!

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.840
To something as elaborate as becoming an&nbsp;
escaped monkey rampaging through the yard.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.960
Would you like to see our reptile section?

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.360
And while most of it can be&nbsp;
written off as just parents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.760
committing to the bit to like the nth degree,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.640
there are other moments in the show&nbsp;
that seem impossible to believe.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.520
Like the episode where the kids literally learn&nbsp;
to use magic to influence their surroundings.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.880
Or when Bingo uses a feather&nbsp;
wand to turn everything heavy.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.920
Cereal heavy!

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:28.760
What's going on?

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.840
And as you really watch the show, more and&nbsp;
more of these strange things keep popping up.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.800
These imaginary games seem to have&nbsp;
a ridiculous amount of influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.840
on both the characters and the world around them.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.760
Enough so that I just kind of have to ask,

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.880
Is magic real in Bluey?

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.400
Are all of these games actually the&nbsp;
result of the characters casting spells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.160
hexes, and charms on each other?

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.720
Well, after digging back into the world of Bluey&nbsp;
and pulling out some classic Theorist tricks,

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.080
I believe that I can definitively&nbsp;
prove the answer to be yes.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.280
Grab your wands and sticky geckos, friends,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.440
as we dig into the bonafide evidence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.120
the magical conspiracy taking&nbsp;
place in the world of Bluey.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.600
Now, on the surface, you might look at a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.440
these games and just think that it's&nbsp;
the imagination of children at work.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.640
That they're just very invested in their games.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.000
But there's commitment, and&nbsp;
then there's just going way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.040
beyond what you'd expect to come&nbsp;
out of real kids at these ages.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.240
Like, to the point that these&nbsp;
games don't feel like a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.600
of toddlers playing make-believe games anymore.

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.880
And this is firmly on display from&nbsp;
the very first episode of the series.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.640
In episode one, Magic Xylophone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.280
we see Bluey and Bingo playing with a&nbsp;
xylophone that can freeze anyone in place.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.600
They just straight up stop,&nbsp;
mid-step and mid-sentence.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.080
At first, Bingo and Bluey&nbsp;
use the xylophone to freeze&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.080
Bandit in place and play some pranks on him.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.400
And he commits to the bit so much that&nbsp;
he's frozen when the kids aren't around.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.160
And the neighbors start giving him funny looks.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.480
Now, to be fair, you could&nbsp;
absolutely just chalk that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.360
up to a good old dad playing along with the kids.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.080
But here's the thing.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:45.880
Later in the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.000
Bingo gets upset because Bluey won't&nbsp;
let her have a turn with the xylophone.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.200
And when she finally gets a hold of it later on&nbsp;
in the episode, Bingo uses it to freeze Bluey.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.000
And it works.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.160
If Bluey truly wants the xylophone as badly&nbsp;
as she does, and doesn't want to play along,

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.240
this seven-year-old girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:02.480
she could just easily break the rules&nbsp;
of the game and take it from Bingo.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.200
But she doesn't.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.960
Instead, she gets a pretty serious&nbsp;
heart-to-heart with Bingo while frozen.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.880
Now, this wouldn't be such a big deal&nbsp;
if this was just a one-off event.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:14.600
But it's not.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.840
This sort of extreme imagination game is&nbsp;
a common occurrence throughout the series.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.600
In the episode, Feather Wand, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.200
this is the one where Bingo uses&nbsp;
a feather to turn things heavy,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.440
we see her turn the toilet seat heavy just as&nbsp;
her dad's getting ready to do his business.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.120
And instead of breaking from the game, he&nbsp;
chooses to run downstairs and relieve himself,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.520
Well, it's unclear where exactly, but&nbsp;
it's implied to be in the front yard,

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.800
based on Chilli's scream and&nbsp;
Bandit greeting the neighbor.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.760
Bandit! What are you doing?

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:41.680
Morning, Wendy!

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.680
I mean, you could be committed to a game, sure.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.800
But in this moment, Bandit's exposing himself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.360
his loved ones and his neighbors&nbsp;
in a completely inappropriate way.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.800
Public indecency is literally a crime.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.400
Are you doing that just for the sake of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.560
imagination game for your&nbsp;
four- and seven-year-old?

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.800
Speaking of being inappropriate, there are also&nbsp;
instances like the season two episode Dance Mode,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:04.629
where the kids are given three chances to&nbsp;
flick one of their parents' tails and say,

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:04.640
Dance Mode!

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.120
which forces the parent to&nbsp;
dance no matter where they are.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.920
Seems like it should be a fun game, right?

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:12.960
Well, it would be if the kids didn't decide to&nbsp;
use it on Chilli when she's crossing the road.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.200
Ooh, shake it, Chilli!

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.320
Morning, Wendy!

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.920
Now, obviously, this is super embarrassing&nbsp;
for Chilli, but look at her dance moves.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.320
If she were truly in full control of her&nbsp;
body and wanted to get out of the situation,

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.080
she could turn away from the crowd of&nbsp;
cars so she doesn't have to face them,

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.240
or use moves that move her further&nbsp;
along in the street faster.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.320
Instead, she's shaking her thing&nbsp;
in front of Wendy's windshield.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:35.680
Wendy apparently is just the recipient of&nbsp;
a lot of these embarrassing Heeler moments.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.360
Now, again, if this was just a one-off&nbsp;
event, we could just ignore it,

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.560
but it happens again to Bandit in the post office.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.240
Uh, are you okay?

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.480
I'm fine. Just ignore this.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:44.960
This right here?

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.320
It's just disrespectful to both the store clerk&nbsp;
and the massive line of people waiting behind him.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.440
Even when the store clerk politely asks&nbsp;
him to stop, Bandit says that he can't.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.640
If you could just stop dancing for a moment.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:57.280
I would if I could, mate.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.440
Oi! Twinkle Toes! Hurry up!

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.120
It's either magic, or the Heelers just suck.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.720
And it's here, when the games start&nbsp;
to affect their everyday lives,

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.040
when you really start to see the&nbsp;
pretend nature of the games break down.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.040
For instance, let's take a look&nbsp;
at Season 3's episode Sheepdog,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.960
where Bandit pretends to be a sheep to distract&nbsp;
the kids away from bothering their mum.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:15.600
But here's the thing.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.480
When Bluey isn't looking, Sheep&nbsp;
Bandit still decides to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.160
into Wendy's yard and start eating her laundry.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:24.840
He even headbutts her in the rear end&nbsp;
when she threatens to bring in Chili.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.320
That right there, again, is a level of&nbsp;
commitment that's either the result of magic,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.640
or just extreme irresponsibility&nbsp;
on the part of Bandit.

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.200
Also, seriously, Wendy has got&nbsp;
to be the most patient neighbor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.400
ever to be living next to these public nuisances.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.600
But the thing that really decides it, is that&nbsp;
even when the kids stop playing the game,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.040
Bandit doesn't drop the act.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:44.840
He is still acting like a sheep 20 minutes later,

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:46.840
while his kids have been upstairs&nbsp;
playing with the neighbor,

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.080
and his wife is just wrapping up her alone time.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.760
Is everything okay?

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.120
Yep, all good here.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.720
Why is he still doing it when everyone&nbsp;
else has moved on from the game?

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.040
But perhaps most compelling&nbsp;
of all is Daddy Drop-Off,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.720
when Bandit has to take the kids&nbsp;
to school and they're running late.

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:03.360
And yet, just as he's running to the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.800
Bingo finds a leaf and uses it&nbsp;
to trap Bandit in slow motion.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.200
Rack him and stack him, kids, we've got gooo...

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.280
If Bandit's late getting his kids to school,

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.000
you'd think that he'd just ignore&nbsp;
the game and get them in the car.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.400
That'd be the responsible thing.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.480
He could make something up about how&nbsp;
school negates the imaginary game,

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.680
or how this leaf isn't actually a magic wand,

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.960
or literally anything so he can do the responsible&nbsp;
thing and get his kids to school on time.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.120
But again, he acts like there isn't a choice here.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.800
He acts like he's been under a magic spell.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.360
The slow motion spell is only released&nbsp;
when Bingo waves the wand again,

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.280
leading to Bandit smacking into his car.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.120
But hold on.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:42.520
Bandit is released from the spell&nbsp;
when Bingo waves the wand, sure,

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.480
but she's standing behind him.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.360
If this were truly a pretend game,

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:49.400
there's no physical way he'd be able&nbsp;
to know when she waved that wand.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.920
We don't even see his eyes moving or&nbsp;
tracking behind him to look at her.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.160
Watch the clip again.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:56.680
In fact, we can actually go one step deeper.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:00.320
Here's the scene in a video editor where&nbsp;
we can see the visuals and audio track.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:00.880
Check this out.

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.120
Bingo saying, "yah"

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.520
and the wand wave sound effect,

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.560
they're right here.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.520
They're actually occurring after the&nbsp;
wand is waved and Bandit's released.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.400
This right here shows us that&nbsp;
there's no way Bandit would know

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.360
to have sped back up again&nbsp;
based on sound or visual cues.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.200
It can only be explained using real magic.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.120
And this is how we know magic&nbsp;
exists in the real world.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:21.240
For real life?

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.320
Yeah, Bluey, for real life.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.920
Because we get physical proof of magic existing.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.000
Like here, where Bingo sneaks into the bathroom,

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.240
heavies Bluey's toothbrush without her hearing,

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.360
and it just magically works.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:36.800
Up to this point, Bluey hasn't&nbsp;
been a part of the game,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:39.120
so she wouldn't know that&nbsp;
things are meant to be heavy.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.280
And while she could just be&nbsp;
pretending to not see or hear Bingo,

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.920
the way her reactions are animated&nbsp;
certainly seems to indicate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.480
Bluey is unaware of anything that's going on.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:49.760
But the piece of evidence that really seals it,

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.280
the one that is irrefutable and definitely&nbsp;
proves that magic exists in this world,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:55.960
happens a bit later that same episode.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:58.680
At the top of the episode, we watch&nbsp;
as Bingo uses the feather wand

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:02.040
to make her dad's cereal&nbsp;
box, bowl, and spoon heavy,

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.480
forcing them all to the floor.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:06.760
And throughout the episode, we check in on&nbsp;
Bandit as he attempts to prepare his breakfast.

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.560
But there is one moment&nbsp;
that absolutely stands out.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.440
What's wrong, Bandit?

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.520
You used to be able to lift spoons.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:16.320
Did you catch it?

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.960
Here, as Bandit tries to use this broom and&nbsp;
rugby ball to pry the spoon off the floor,

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.480
the broom physically bends.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.440
And that ball is squeezed under&nbsp;
the heavy weight of the spoon.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.240
And we see the broom bend starting at the spoon.

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.560
There is no way that this could happen&nbsp;
if the spoon was at its normal weight.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.240
Literally no physical way for that to happen.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:37.000
Therefore, the feather wand actually&nbsp;
had to have worked on that spoon,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.000
giving us even more definitive proof that&nbsp;
magic actually exists in the Bluey-verse.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.840
And that suddenly puts other&nbsp;
episodes into a whole new context.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.840
For instance, in the Season 1 episode, Fairies,

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.080
the Heeler household is bombarded with&nbsp;
shenanigans brought about by the dreaded fairies.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.640
You know what this means?

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:53.440
What?

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.200
We've got fairies!

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.920
Now, a lot of this episode is framed&nbsp;
as Bandit trying to make up to Bingo

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.560
for ignoring her earlier in the day.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.800
And on a surface level, you&nbsp;
might just watch this episode

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.720
and think that the parents are making&nbsp;
the magic happen for their kids.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:09.600
But for as much as the parents here could have&nbsp;
made some of the things in the episode happen,

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:11.520
there is no way they did all of it.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.280
For instance, midway through the&nbsp;
episode, everyone's hiding in the closet

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:17.520
and Bingo is tasked with finding clues&nbsp;
as to what the fairies might want

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:18.800
in order to leave them alone.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.680
When she returns sometime later, the&nbsp;
house's sitting room is a disaster.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.920
Toys and trash are scattered everywhere.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.880
Figurines are hanging from the ceiling.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.240
Koalas? Just chillin'.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.080
Like I said, this is an Australian show.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.760
It's implied that Bingo was the one&nbsp;
doing all this, but there's just no way.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:36.880
In order to hang those koalas from the walls&nbsp;
and that shoe in the middle of the room,

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.400
she would have needed a stepladder that's&nbsp;
at minimum eight times her own height.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.400
And for as much as Chilli and Bandit disregard&nbsp;
the needs and safety of their fellow neighbors,

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.760
I don't think that even they would&nbsp;
allow their favorite four-year-old

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.000
to use something like that unsupervised.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.200
But still, that little bit's happening off-camera.

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:56.600
What about something that's&nbsp;
happening on-camera in real time?

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.040
That'd be pretty convincing, right?

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.080
Well, in that very same episode, after discovering&nbsp;
a fairy ring with Bandit's phone inside,

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.400
they turn around and find that the entire&nbsp;
yard has been covered in fairy rings.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.080
There is no way that Bandit and&nbsp;
Chilli could have set all of these up.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.560
We see them in literally every&nbsp;
scene leading up to this big reveal.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.120
And if those rings had already been there,&nbsp;
Bingo and Bluey would have already seen them.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.120
And if they're just pretending to not see them,

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.320
you wouldn't get a Bingo&nbsp;
reaction looking like this.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:22.000
Her eyes are huge.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.200
She is shooketh by those rings.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.080
But we don't just have to assume here either.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.280
Again, we get physical proof that&nbsp;
they weren't there a second ago.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.200
While the camera does its&nbsp;
best to try and frame out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:32.760
the rest of the yard for the bulk of the scene,

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.720
look at where Bluey's standing&nbsp;
relative to that first fairy ring.

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:39.640
If you map her position out relative&nbsp;
to the wide shot that we later cut to,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.880
technically we should have been&nbsp;
able to see that tablet fairy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.200
ring in the bottom right corner of the screen.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:45.000
But it's not there.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.560
Until Bluey notices all the rest&nbsp;
of the fairy rings across the yard.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.200
This had to have been done using magic.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:54.080
So, am I saying that fairies&nbsp;
exist in the Bluey-verse?

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.080
You bet I am!

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.040
At the end of the episode, Bingo straight&nbsp;
up sees a fairy setting up her dominoes

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.440
in the same heart shape&nbsp;
that Bingo made for her dad.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.200
While it could just be imaginary, and the show&nbsp;
tries to leave it open for interpretation,

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.920
all the physical proof we have from bending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:09.760
brooms to fairy rings to slow&nbsp;
motion wands prove otherwise.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:13.960
And now that we have a couple undeniable pieces&nbsp;
of proof that magic exists here, so what?

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.640
I mean, that's crazy and all, but&nbsp;
why should we care about this?

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:20.640
Well, we can actually track the source&nbsp;
of the magic, and it's none other than...

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.480
If you look back through all&nbsp;
the episodes and evidence,

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.400
you'll realize that many of the imaginary&nbsp;
games played throughout the series

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:28.920
are actually all created by&nbsp;
Chilli using some form of magic.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:29.520
Some examples?

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:33.080
In Asparagus, Chilli gives Bluey&nbsp;
a piece of asparagus and explains,

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:37.080
It's a magic asparagus. It turns&nbsp;
people into any animal you want.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:41.760
Chilli's whispering this to Bluey, so Bandit and&nbsp;
Bingo can't hear her and know what the game is.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.720
And yet, when Bluey shouts,

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.000
Donkey!

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.120
Bandit immediately turns into a donkey.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:51.680
Chilli's created a magic item and is teaching her&nbsp;
kids to do the same all throughout the series.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:56.480
Magic that, again, goes a bit too far when Bluey&nbsp;
transforms her dad from a peacock to a walrus

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:59.640
and causes him to tumble out&nbsp;
of a tree, landing on his back.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.120
That's not playing along for the funsies.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.720
That is risking severe personal injury.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:05.560
Additionally, Chilli's often shown to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:08.240
control the magic within the games&nbsp;
that Bluey and Bingo come up with.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.760
In the episode Magic, when Chilli sees&nbsp;
Bluey and Bingo using magic on Bandit,

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.960
the kids run and she's able to stop them.

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:15.760
Who taught you how to use magic?

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:19.640
In fact, Bluey just straight up confirms&nbsp;
that she learned to use her magic from Mum.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.000
You use magic?

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.000
Magic?

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.520
Yeah, you can use it to make people do anything.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:25.320
Mum taught me.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:26.600
Honestly, I could go on and on.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:28.280
Like in the ending of that very same episode,

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:31.760
where Bingo uses magic to sit her dad&nbsp;
down and force her mum to the door,

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:33.440
all without Dad looking.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.520
Regardless, I think that the one&nbsp;
big key takeaway of all of this

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:39.120
is whether it's extreme pretend or real magic,

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.840
the Heelers would suck to&nbsp;
have in your neighborhood.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.640
Oh sure, this magical family&nbsp;
may seem charming to all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.720
us happiness-starved children of the internet

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:48.400
for their love and commitment to play,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.720
but that's us looking in from the&nbsp;
outside, removed from the situation.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.480
As we've seen here today, this&nbsp;
one family disrupts the flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.560
of daily life for literally everyone around them.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.280
Whether it's in the line at the post office,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:01.840
or when you're trying to dry&nbsp;
your laundry in the front yard,

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:06.120
or when your name is Lucky's dad and you're&nbsp;
getting mauled by magical crocodiles,

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.120
Get it off me!

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:08.640
attacked by magical snakes,

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.000
Snakes!

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.120
Get it off me!

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.080
or just gnawed by lions.

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:14.716
Aw, Mrs. Heeler,

00:14:14.716 --> 00:14:16.040
I dunno know about this.

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.320
Imagine how it would feel to&nbsp;
have your adult neighbor pop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:20.400
out of a bush and start gnawing at your foot.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.520
There's childlike whimsy&nbsp;
and spreading a joy, sure,

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.600
and then there's assault of your fellow human.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:26.800
Uh, fellow dog creature thing.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:29.640
So while it might seem to be all&nbsp;
fun and games for Bluey and Bingo,

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.080
living close to these monsters&nbsp;
would be literally anything but.

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:35.480
But hey, that's just a theory.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:37.480
A film theory!

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:39.289
And cut!

